   "summary": "Timing, scope, methods yet to be decided As concerns grow over the working conditions of teenage K-pop idols after an article on the topic in the New York Times early this month, the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism said Tuesday that it will look into how well the basic rights of underage entertainers are protected next year. 5 report by The New York Times, which found that the contract girl group NewJeans signed as minors omitted key protections for underage performers.",
   "summary_zh": "时间、范围和方法尚待确定。鉴于《纽约时报》本月早些时候刊登有关青少年偶像歌手工作环境的文章后，社会对相关工作条件的担忧日益加剧，韩国文化、体育和旅游部周二表示，明年将调查未成年艺人的基本权利得到了多大程度的保护。《纽约时报》的这篇5篇报道发现，女子团体NewJeans在成员仍未成年时签订的合同遗漏了针对未成年表演者的关键保障条款。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Timing, scope, methods yet to be decided As concerns grow over the working conditions of teenage K-pop idols after an article on the topic in the New York Times early this month, the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism said Tuesday that it will look into how well the basic rights of underage entertainers are protected next year. The move comes amid mounting scrutiny of the industry following a Nov. 5 report by The New York Times, which found that the contract girl group NewJeans signed as minors omitted key protections for underage performers. “We plan to conduct a fact-finding survey on the status of underage entertainers in 2027, but its timing, scope and methods have yet to be decided,” Choi Hye-ji, a public relations officer at the ministry, told The Korea Times. The official, however, drew a line between the survey and the controversy, saying that the decision to take the measure was not triggered by the news report alone. The newspaper obtained the exclusive contract the five NewJeans members signed in 2022 with Ador, a label under HYBE, when they were between 14 and 17. According to the report, the contract left out provisions from the government’s standard contract that protect minors’ physical and mental health, education, sleep and rest. The report also cited accounts that the members often practiced until 2 a.m. as trainees, and said four of the five had dropped out of school. HYBE rebutted the report, saying the contract was lawfully concluded and that the members’ rights were sufficiently protected through a supplementary agreement for minors. NewJeans debuted in 2022 to massive success, but became embroiled in a dispute with HYBE after the ouster of subsidiary Ador’s then-CEO Min Hee-jin in 2024. After a court upheld the validity of their contract, four of the members returned to the agency, though the group has yet to resume activities. Under the Popular Culture and Arts Industry Development Act, agencies signing contracts with minors must include measures guaranteeing their health, education, sleep and rest. In principle, minors are barred from working between 10 p.m. and 6 a.m. The law’s protections cover a growing number of idols who debut in their early teens, such as the members of rookie girl group A2O LITE, whose average age is just under 15",
