   "title": "Korea joins NATO-led Starlift space initiative to boost launch capabilities",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/foreignaffairs/others/20261117/korea-joins-nato-led-starlift-space-initiative-to-boost-launch-capabilities",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Tue, 17 Nov 2026 08:57:02 GMT",
   "_dt": 1794905822.0,
   "ageDays": 0.02,
   "summary": "Korea has joined a space initiative led by the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) in an effort to secure rapid and reliable satellite launch capabilities, the defense ministry said Tuesday. Joining the program will bolster Korea's reliable access to space by diversifying the military's launch options and expand opportunities for the domestic space industry in overseas markets, it said in a release.",
   "summary_zh": "韩国国防部周二表示，韩国已加入由北大西洋公约组织（NATO）牵头的太空计划，旨在确保快速、可靠的卫星发射能力。国防部在一份声明中说，加入该计划将通过增加军方发射选择的多样性，强化韩国可靠进入太空的能力，并为韩国国内太空产业拓展海外市场创造更多机会。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Korea has joined a space initiative led by the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO) in an effort to secure rapid and reliable satellite launch capabilities, the defense ministry said Tuesday. The ministry signed a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with NATO on the Starlift program in Brussels on Oct. 29, becoming the first non-NATO member country to join the initiative, according to the ministry. Joining the program will bolster Korea's reliable access to space by diversifying the military's launch options and expand opportunities for the domestic space industry in overseas markets, it said in a release. \"In particular, gaining access to participating nations' launch vehicles and space ports will allow Korea to reduce its dependence on specific foreign launch providers,\" it added. The Starlift initiative was launched in October 2024 as a multinational \"space alliance\" aimed at helping participating nations launch space assets at short notice by utilizing their launch vehicles and space ports. In contingencies, it aims to ensure that enemy attacks on space assets would not cripple ground-based military and communications networks. The program is widely seen as a move to deter growing threats from China and Russia. According to NATO's website, the framework initially launched with 14 NATO member states, including the United States, Britain, Germany and the Netherlands, before Canada joined in July of this year. Media reports have said NATO had proposed its four Indo-Pacific partners — Korea, Japan, Australia and New Zealand — to join the initiative in a bid to expand the network",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
