   "title": "2027 AGM season will show whether shareholder reforms work: Terton Capital",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/economy/20261117/2027-agm-season-will-show-whether-shareholder-reforms-work-terton-capital",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Tue, 17 Nov 2026 08:57:02 GMT",
   "_dt": 1794905822.0,
   "ageDays": 0.02,
   "summary": "Next year's annual general meeting (AGM) season will serve as a key test of new reforms designed to strengthen minority shareholder rights, potentially attracting or deterring foreign investment in Korea, according to Ryan Albert, founder and managing member of Terton Capital.",
   "summary_zh": "Terton Capital创始人兼管理成员Ryan Albert表示，明年的年度股东大会季将成为检验旨在加强少数股东权利的新改革的关键考验，这些改革可能吸引或阻碍对韩国的外国投资。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Next year's annual general meeting (AGM) season will serve as a key test of new reforms designed to strengthen minority shareholder rights, potentially attracting or deterring foreign investment in Korea, according to Ryan Albert, founder and managing member of Terton Capital. \"There are a lot of investors looking at the market,\" Albert said at a press conference in Seoul Tuesday. \"The 2027 AGM season will be a great test case for some of the new reforms that have been passed.\" While Albert welcomed government reforms aimed at addressing the \"Korea discount,\" or the persistent undervaluation of Korean stocks, he stressed that companies themselves must also take action. Terton Capital has pursued shareholder activism campaigns involving Golfzon Holdings, Orion and Dong-A Pharmaceutical. It has urged Golfzon Holdings to distribute 250 billion won in proceeds from the sale of Golfzon County to shareholders, opposed Orion's proposed acquisition of JoongAng Ilbo and pressed Dong-A Pharmaceutical to implement five governance and capital allocation reforms by next March's annual general meeting. Albert said Korea's efforts to improve corporate governance are still in the early innings, emphasizing that higher corporate returns could benefit not only companies but also households and the National Pension Service. Terton Capital is a U.S.-based investment firm launched in May that focuses on long-term investments in publicly listed equities across the Asia-Pacific region. Korean stocks account for the vast majority of its portfolio, with the firm holding stakes in more than 10 Korean companies",
