   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The national capital woke up to its coolest morning of the season so far on Tuesday (November 17, 2026), with the minimum temperature at Safdarjung dropping to 11.6 degrees Celsius, the lowest recorded this season and also this month. Delhi’s early winter air pollution highest in 3 years: Study Among other weather stations, Palam recorded a minimum temperature of 12.3 degrees Celsius, 0.6 degrees below normal, while Lodhi Road recorded 12 degrees Celsius, one degree above normal.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅后即可解锁这些内容。首都于周二（2026年11月17日）迎来入秋以来最凉爽的早晨safdarjung最低气温降至11.6摄氏度，为本季及本月有记录以来的最低值。研究显示，德里初冬空气污染达到三年来最高水平。在其他气象站中，帕勒姆最低气温为12.3摄氏度，比正常水平低0.6摄氏度；洛迪路最低气温为12摄氏度，比正常水平高1摄氏度。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The national capital woke up to its coolest morning of the season so far on Tuesday (November 17, 2026), with the minimum temperature at Safdarjung dropping to 11.6 degrees Celsius, the lowest recorded this season and also this month. Delhi’s early winter air pollution highest in 3 years: Study Among other weather stations, Palam recorded a minimum temperature of 12.3 degrees Celsius, 0.6 degrees below normal, while Lodhi Road recorded 12 degrees Celsius, one degree above normal. The Ridge station recorded 14.6 degrees Celsius, 1.1 degrees above normal, and Ayanagar recorded 12.6 degrees Celsius, 0.1 degrees below normal. The maximum temperature is expected to settle at 27 degrees Celsius, with moderate fog forecast during the day, according to the India Meteorological Department (IMD). The city's air quality index (AQI) stood at 238 at 8 a.m., in the 'poor' category, according to the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB). Of the 58 monitoring stations that recorded data, 32 were in the 'poor' category, 16 in the 'moderate' category and 10 in the 'very poor' category. Sirifort recorded the worst air quality, with an AQI of 333. According to the CPCB's classification, an AQI between 0 and 50 is considered 'good', 51 and 100 'satisfactory', 101 and 200 'moderate', 201 and 300 'poor', 301 and 400 'very poor', and 401 and 500 'severe'. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
