   "summary": "South Korea's presidential office said Sunday it is considering life imprisonment without parole as part of an initiative aimed at strengthening public safety and cracking down on crimes targeting vulnerable people. In 2023, the Cabinet approved a bill to amend the Criminal Act to introduce life imprisonment without parole as a means of permanently isolating perpetrators of heinous crimes from society.",
   "summary_zh": "韩国总统府星期日表示，正在考虑将不得假释的终身监禁纳入一项旨在加强公共安全、打击针对弱势群体犯罪的计划。2023年，内阁批准了一项《刑法》修正案法案，拟引入不得假释的终身监禁，使犯下滔天罪行者永久与社会隔离。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "South Korea's presidential office said Sunday it is considering life imprisonment without parole as part of an initiative aimed at strengthening public safety and cracking down on crimes targeting vulnerable people. The initiative will focus on crimes targeting socially vulnerable people, including relationship-based crimes and crimes with apparently random motives. The government will also step up efforts to eradicate crimes involving scams, illegal drugs, illegal private lending, digital sex crimes and false or fabricated information, among other offenses that disrupt people's daily lives or threaten public order, presidential chief of staff Lim Kwang-hyun said. Lim said the initiative follows instructions from President Lee Jae Myung at a Cabinet meeting last week to concentrate government-wide resources on protecting people's lives and making daily life safer. \"In cases involving economic harm that exploit people's desperate circumstances, we will pursue those responsible even overseas, dismantle the criminal operations at their source and block criminal proceeds,\" Lim said at a briefing. Under current law, inmates serving life sentences may be considered for parole after serving 20 years. In 2023, the Cabinet approved a bill to amend the Criminal Act to introduce life imprisonment without parole as a means of permanently isolating perpetrators of heinous crimes from society. The proposal would have allowed courts to distinguish between life sentences with and without the possibility of parole. The bill failed to pass the 21st National Assembly before its term expired in May 2024",
